# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Our Story -- Firebase Configuration
# Copy this file to .env.local and fill in your credentials.
# NEVER commit .env.local to git.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Go to https://console.firebase.google.com → Your project → Project settings → Your apps
VITE_FIREBASE_API_KEY=your_firebase_api_key_here
VITE_FIREBASE_AUTH_DOMAIN=your-project.firebaseapp.com
VITE_FIREBASE_PROJECT_ID=your-project-id
VITE_FIREBASE_STORAGE_BUCKET=your-project.appspot.com
VITE_FIREBASE_MESSAGING_SENDER_ID=123456789
VITE_FIREBASE_APP_ID=1:123456789:web:abc123

# Push notifications (optional — the app works fine without this, you just
# won't get notified when it's closed). Go to Project settings → Cloud
# Messaging → Web configuration → Web Push certificates → Generate key pair.
VITE_FIREBASE_VAPID_KEY=

# Real Firebase Auth passwords for the two partner accounts. These are NOT
# the birthday you type into the app's own login screen — that's just this
# app's local "who are you" gate (see src/store/useAuthStore.ts). The actual
# Firebase Auth credential must be a real secret, generated once and never
# derived from anything in this repo, because this repo is public: a
# password computed from a public birthday is a password anyone reading
# GitHub can compute too. Generate 32+ random characters each, e.g.:
#   openssl rand -base64 24
VITE_FIREBASE_HIM_PASSWORD=
VITE_FIREBASE_HER_PASSWORD=

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# SETUP INSTRUCTIONS (one-time, takes ~10 minutes)
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# 1. Go to firebase.google.com → Create project "our-story" → Continue
#    (Disable Google Analytics, not needed)
#
# 2. Add a Web app → nickname "our-story-web" → Register app
#    Copy the config values into this file above.
#
# 3. Authentication
#    Left sidebar → Authentication → Sign-in method → Email/Password → Enable → Save
#
# 4. Firestore Database
#    Left sidebar → Firestore Database → Create database
#    Choose: Production mode → Next
#    Location: africa-south1 (Johannesburg) → Enable
#    Rules are NOT pasted here by hand — they live in firestore.rules at the
#    repo root and deploy with:
#      firebase deploy --only firestore:rules
#    That file restricts access to exactly the two partner accounts by email.
#    Do not replace it with a rule that grants access to any authenticated
#    user — that lets anyone who self-registers a Firebase account read and
#    write all of this app's data.
#
# 5. Storage
#    Left sidebar → Storage → Get started → Production mode → Next
#    Same region (africa-south1) → Done
#    Rules likewise live in storage.rules at the repo root and deploy with:
#      firebase deploy --only storage
#
# 6. Push notifications (optional, needed only for background/closed-app
#    message + call alerts)
#    - Requires the Blaze (pay-as-you-go) billing plan — Cloud Functions
#      don't run at all on the free Spark plan. Settings → Usage and
#      billing → Modify plan. Real usage cost for two people: ~$0/month.
#    - Project settings → Cloud Messaging → Web configuration →
#      Web Push certificates → Generate key pair → paste into
#      VITE_FIREBASE_VAPID_KEY above.
#    - Deploy the Cloud Function that actually sends the pushes:
#        cd functions && npm install && cd ..
#        firebase deploy --only functions
#
# 7. Generate VITE_FIREBASE_HIM_PASSWORD / VITE_FIREBASE_HER_PASSWORD above
#    (openssl rand -base64 24, or any password manager) and set the same
#    values on the two accounts in Authentication → Users, or let the app
#    create them on first run — it signs up with whatever is in these two
#    env vars the first time each account doesn't yet exist.
#
# That is it! Run: npm run dev
#
# Everything (chat, letters, dreams, memories, photos) syncs through Firebase.
